---
/* The shape of the bill — for "What an autonomous run actually costs".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two bands, two of the article's arguments. The first: the lanes — nearly
   the whole bill is the one agent doing the work, the advisor lane is
   pennies, the gates a rounding line. The second: what a token even is — the
   corpus's tokens are overwhelmingly cache reads, the session re-reading what
   it already knew, which is why session length dominates every knob an
   orchestrator can turn.

   The bar widths are schematic on purpose and the caption says so: drawing
   them to the corpus's proportions would make the drawing a figure, and every
   figure on this site has to come out of the strip, where it is recomputed.
   Same idiom as FigureLoop: panes, hairlines, mono labels; the quiet roles do
   the work here, because the subject is how much of the bill is quiet. */
---

<figure class="fig-cost">
  <div
    class="bands"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Two bands. The lanes: the agent doing the work is nearly the whole bill; the advisor lane on a smaller model is pennies; the gate battery is a rounding line. The tokens: almost everything the corpus moved was cache reads — the session re-reading what it already knew — with fresh tokens in and out a thin slice."
  >
    <div class="band" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="b-label">the lanes — who spent it</p>
      <div class="bars">
        <div class="bar agent">
          <span class="name">agent</span>
          <span class="note">the one doing the work — nearly the whole bill</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bar advisor">
          <span class="name">advisor</span>
          <span class="note">a smaller model, reading and commenting — pennies</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bar gates">
          <span class="name">gates</span>
          <span class="note">the battery — a rounding line</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="band" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="b-label">the tokens — what was actually moved</p>
      <div class="split">
        <span class="cache">cache reads — the session re-reading what it already knew</span>
        <span class="fresh">fresh in and out</span>
      </div>
      <p class="b-note">
        which is why session length dominates every other variable: a longer
        session re-reads a longer past, every turn, at the meter's pace
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The shape of the bill, not its figures — the widths here are schematic,
    and the measured shares are in the strip. An orchestrator is not many
    expensive models in a trench coat; it is one expensive model, a cheap
    second opinion, and a great deal of re-reading.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig-cost {
    margin: 0;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-l);
    inline-size: min(100%, var(--measure-wide));
  }

  .bands {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  .band {
    background: var(--mantle);
    border: var(--rule);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-m) var(--space-s);
  }

  .b-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-m);
  }

  .bars {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .bar {
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
  }

  /* Schematic widths: an order, not a measurement. The agent lane runs the
     full measure; the advisor and gate lanes are visibly slivers without
     pretending to be percentages of anything. */
  .bar.advisor {
    inline-size: min(100%, 14rem);
  }

  .bar.gates {
    inline-size: min(100%, 18rem);
  }

  .bar .name {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .bar .note {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* The token split: one strip, two segments. The cache segment wears the
     quiet surface role because the subject is how much of the bill is the
     quiet part; the fresh slice is the accent, being the only part a reader
     would have guessed was the bill. */
  .split {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  .cache {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .fresh {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    color: var(--current);
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .fresh {
      writing-mode: horizontal-tb;
      font-size: var(--text-small);
    }
  }

  .b-note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
